too many libertarians have been  terrified because they're afraid well I  might make a compromise and that would  make me dirty hi I'm Michael Moynihan  with Reason TV and today we are with Tom  G Palmer the author of realizing freedom  libertarian theory history and practice  a senior fellow at the Cato Institute  and vice president for international  programs at the Atlas Economic Research  Foundation  Tom welcome well thank you it's a  pleasure to be here  I give our viewers a an impression of  what you're trying to achieve with this  book what the sort of premise in this  book is I wanted to present a kind of  coherent picture of libertarian ideas  with two elements to it one is it's  important to respond to our critics so I  have some very appointed responses to  communitarians extrema gala Tyrians  socialist conservative communitarians  and so on because I think too many  occasions in the past defenders of  Liberty have retired from the field and  I think you need to push back the other  element was to show that Liberty is  about doing it's about implementing  freedom it's about realizing freedom not  just talking about or having seminars  about it those are important but it's  about going out and doing the hard work  of changing the world to realize a more  free more just society first piece in  this book that I saw was 1981 that's for  the sort of starting point is how are we  doing now in the period from when you  started writing these pieces to where we  are right now last couple years have  been really bad especially in the United  States the Bush administration now Obama  or the bowl bomber administration as I'd  like to think of it has been a  catastrophe but if you go back to 1981  there has been tremendous improvement  there's no more Soviet Union the Warsaw  Pact is is finished there's been this  worldwide spread of an awareness of  Liberty the internet communications have  generally had a very very positive  impact so I think that even though last  couple years have been really bad we're  living at one of the most free times in  human history but it's fragile this sort  of thing can be unwound and we should  look at the failures of the classical  liberals in the 19th century to prepare  for the 20th I worry that we might  become complacent in our Liberty and not  think about what is at stake that it is  in fact quite fragile  the rise of protectionism President  Obama just this week did something  unbelievably stupid and is now set off a  trade war with China he's at the tire  tariffs right yeah  and now the Chinese are talking about  chicken tariffs and odd will be a part  tariffs and so on nothing good will come  of this  it's very very dangerous and we need to  act quickly to defeat protectionism and  to assert the moral superiority of free  trade and free markets not just it's  more efficient it is it is morally  better and we need to take to the field  and take these people on you have a very  international perspective your pieces on  Russia and the Middle East it's Center  Latin America what can we be doing right  now to sort of help spread freedom in a  time where as you point out it's kind of  in a precarious situation that's a  really hard thing because they're kind  of very few a place to place but I'm  involved in 17 projects from 17  different languages Russian Chinese  Portuguese bahasa Arabic Persian and so  on and we're very active in all of those  with summer schools seminars teaching  getting people to understand these key  points Leonard read the founder of fie  had a good point he says we can't be  effective if we don't understand the  philosophy of Liberty and the  institutional foundations and rule of  law property limited government  toleration and so on but then also you  need to do something that's even harder  it's risky you need to take risks to be  willing to make proposals for real  change for free trade eliminating  tariffs which i think is really one of  the most important things this is the  time to push the free trade agenda the  Liberals of the 18th and 19th centuries  understood free trade leads to peace the  academic and scholarly research is now  overwhelming on this protectionism sets  the stage for war so we should be  proposing a rash radical free trade  agenda and this is something I've been  really promoting all over the world and  there are free trade campaigns now being  launched in India and Central  Asia in China Latin America and  elsewhere that's very important but then  the other thing is don't be so fearful  of taking risks becoming involved in the  process of policy change including  politics when that's appropriate in  various countries too many libertarians  have been terrified because they're  afraid while I might make a compromise  and that would make me dirty  well the question is do you want to  change the world you want to move toward  a freer world then you have to engage it  and you can't be in this little corner  saying well we're going to be secure and  ineffective ineffective people usually  feel very good about themselves it's the  people who affect change we're the ones  who have doubts who make mistakes but  that's how you do it you have to take  risks and it's obviously gonna differ  from place to place our colleagues in  China understand where the line is that  gets you arrested and they try and get  up really close to it but generally  better not to end up in prison  but you want to move where that line is  and it has been moving over time  partly because one of those things that  is helping push that line as you  mentioned is trade right now posts  financial crises do you see a problem do  you see us backsliding in any ways on  trade I mean obviously you mentioned  bama's tire tariffs we have of course  the NAFTA issues with Mexican trucks on  the road etc is this a difficult time  when you say right now is the time for  more free trade i remember what rahm  emanuel said you never want a serious  crisis to go to waste and that's what  all the protectionists are saying right  now and they are really geared up the  consequence of those policies would be a  repeat of smoot-hawley crash the world  economy 1930 Hoover signs that bill US  foreign export trade collapsed as a  consequence within two years total  volume of world trade was down by  two-thirds that's a substantial amount  of what the Great Depression was now we  run that risk again and talking about a  time when there  a full frontal assault by the Naomi  Klein's of the world on free markets on  classical liberalism this seems a very  important piece give us an idea what the  most common misperception that that you  tackle here and you know why it's  important that we fight back against  these things one of the issues that I  address is whether the market is immoral  and that's an important underlining  element of this attack people in  business only care about profits they  don't care about other people they don't  care about others  so that's fundamentally wrong markets  rests on respect for other people I  could just go out and take stuff I'm a  fairly strong guy and I'm pretty  aggressive and I'm fairly tough if I  wanted to I could go take things from  people I don't do that because I respect  them that's what property rights means  and if I want something someone else has  I induced them to give it to me  now you can beg that's not generally  very effective you can give them  something else that they want more so  the market economy rests fundamentally  on respect for other people it's also  the case that there's nothing  particularly selfish about the market  there's been a misunderstanding of Adam  Smith in this regard people going to the  market are partly concerned about others  partly concerned about themselves but  either even mother Teresa tried to get  the lowest price for blankets and  medicines to help other people the  market was what made possible the  charity of the charitable people as well  because they want to economize they want  to find the lowest price as HB act and a  British philosopher put it he says  running a business at a loss is an  especially stupid way of being  charitable what is the future of freedom  and in in the context of your book  realizing freedom how do we realize  freedom now in a in a situation that  that look so grim especially for  economic freedom well first off it's not  all the same every place in the world  looks like the Germans might end up with  a little bit of belt-tightening in their  government they have not taken some of  the crazy measures that we've seen  proposed in this country maybe just  turning on the printing presses but a  little bit more restrained in some of  Europe I think that we're in for a very  tough slog but if people make it  that they do not go along with us and  one thing that's come up is is this  so-called public option that was a term  that was focus-grouped  as they say they went through every name  for socialism and people didn't like it  so this public option people that that  sounds ok whatever that is it's  socialism it's a state-run medical  system and do not believe for a minute  that if we get that it's really going to  be self-financing  that's rubbish and everybody knows it  this is a time for people to stay in a  per say no way and even to be angry  about it I'm not opposed to anger when  injustice and foolishness is being  proposed so I'm all in favor being angry  in the United States about the direction  the country is taking I think they  should have been angry three years ago  when Bush was doing it Tom Palmer author  of most recently realizing freedom  libertarian theory history and practice  thank you for joining us thank you Mike

